{{also|noggen}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Of uncertain origin; attested since the 1600s (e.g. in ''The Tincker of Turvey'') in several forms including the still-current Irish English form {{m|en|naggin}}, the rare older Irish, Scottish and Northern English form ''noggan'', used by Jonathan Swift, and the [[Wexford]] form ''nuggeen''.<ref name="OED">{{R:OED}}</ref> <ref>{{R:EDD}}</ref> Tomás S. Ó Máille and some older dictionary's like Skeat's derive it from {{cog|ga|naigín}}, {{m|ga|cnaigín}}, from {{m|ga|cnagaire}}, {{m|ga|cnag}},<ref>Tomás S. Ó Máille, ''Seanfhocla Chonnacht'', Cois Life, 2010, pag 368</ref><ref>Walter William Skeat, ''A Concise Etymological Dictionary of the English Language'' (1882), page 233</ref> but the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' argues that {{cog|ga|naigín}} and {{cog|gd|noigean}} instead derive from English.<ref name="OED"/> Compare {{m|en|nog}}.

